<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">London Bridge Is Falling Down</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">17th century; first printed c. 1744</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Bridge Is Falling Down&quot;, also known as &quot;My Fair Lady&quot; or &quot;London Bridge&quot;, is a traditional English nursery rhyme and singing game about the decay of London Bridge and the attempts, real or fanciful, to rebuild it.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Similar rhymes are older than the English records: Knippelsbro Går Op og Ned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, Die Magdeburger Brück in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, Pont chus in 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Le porte in 14th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and the English rhyme may have been adapted from one of them to fit the country's best-known bridge. In the comedy The London Chaunticleres, printed in 1657 but probably written about 1636, a dairy woman recalls having danced the building of London Bridge at the Whitsun Ales. Henry Carey used a line of it in his satire Namby Pamby in 1725, and the earliest printed version appeared in Tommy Thumb's Pretty Song Book, printed by John Newbery in London.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The words vary widely. In the usual form the bridge is built up again with one material after another, iron bars that bend and break among them, most of which fail to keep it standing. The verses are quatrains in trochaic metre and rely on repetition rather than rhyme. A tune is printed under the title in John Playford's The Dancing Master of 1718, but it differs from the modern melody, which was first recorded in 1879 in A. H. Rosewig's Illustrated National Songs and Games in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>In the game that goes with it, two players form an arch with their arms and lower it at the end of the song to catch a player passing beneath; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> those caught often go on to a tug of war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The meaning is uncertain. One suggestion links the rhyme to the destruction of London Bridge by Olaf II in 1014 or 1009, but the Norse verse that seemed to echo it was a free translation by Samuel Laing in 1844, modelled on the nursery rhyme itself. Alice Bertha Gomme proposed that it recalls children buried in the foundations of the bridge, a theory later discussed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> and Peter Opie. Others connect it to the repeated damage and repair of Old London Bridge.</p><p>The fair lady has been identified with the Virgin Mary, Matilda of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, Eleanor of Provence and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-lea">River Lea</a>, among others. By the late 19th century the rhyme was among the best known in the English-speaking world, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/t-s-eliot">T. S. Eliot</a> used it at the climax of The Waste Land in 1922.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “London Bridge Is Falling Down” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>London Bridge Is Falling Down</b>&quot;, also known as &quot;My Fair Lady&quot; or &quot;London Bridge&quot;, is a traditional English nursery rhyme and singing game about the decay of London Bridge and the attempts, real or fanciful, to rebuild it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/london-bridge-is-falling-down">London Bridge Is Falling Down</a></li><li><blockquote>The child who is under the arch when the song ends is caught, much as in the English-language game &quot;<b>London Bridge Is Falling Down</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/toryanse">Tōryanse</a></li></ul></section>
